    "reasoning_content": "Now the third paper: need the full text of the PDF/bitstream for quotes. Also RSC page for p3:activity. Let's fetch the bitstream and RSC page.\n\nAlso need to check p3:step quote — the recorded quote \"In this study, we benchmark ML models for the prediction of the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of lubricant-relevant organic liquids, and use MD simulations on a representative subset as an independent, physics-based assessment of the same properties.\" Need to verify it appears on the spiral entity page? The entity page abstract doesn't contain that sentence. Hmm. The step source is the spiral entity page, quote may be in the PDF. Let me check the bitstream.\n\nAlso for p2 items: need \"four final candidates\" quote — yes present: \"From these, we selected four final candidates for experimental verification.\" Good.\n\np2:c1: quote present exactly. Good. But does it support \"transport properties like viscosity and thermal conductivity that their instruments cannot measure are taken from literature\"? The page says \"For other properties that could not be measured with our available instrumentation, we surveyed the literature\". Also \"viscosity values for most candidates were lower...\" relative to benchmark. But are viscosity/thermal conductivity actually measured? Fig 3d includes viscosity? The paper's Table 1 includes dynamic viscosity and thermal conductivity as FOM calculations. The quote supports literature survey for other properties not measured. Does it say viscosity/thermal conductivity specifically? Not explicitly. But Fig 3d includes viscosity (the text mentions \"viscosity values for most candidates were lower than those of the benchmark\"). Hmm, whether viscosity was measured experimentally or taken from literature? Unclear. Partial maybe. The claim: \"Validation today is experimental (TGA, DSC) plus literature values; transport properties like viscosity and thermal conductivity that their instruments cannot measure are taken from the literature rather than computed by MD.\" The page: TGA, DSC measured; other properties surveyed from literature. Viscosity/thermal conductivity are among the other properties presumably (they don't have instruments). Actually they mention viscosity values in results. Hmm. The specific claim that instruments cannot measure viscosity/thermal conductivity is an inference. The quote supports the general structure. I'd call PARTIAL: the page confirms TGA/DSC plus literature for unmeasurable properties, but does not specifically say viscosity/thermal conductivity came from literature rather than experiment. Still, maybe SUPPORTED since the key factual claim about the pipeline is that no MD was used. The card's claim about MD absence — page mentions no MD. I'd lean PARTIAL carefully.\n\np2:c2: screening at billion scale, narrows to four experimentally verified. The quote says 41 million purchasable + 2.54 billion in-silico, 356 Criterion 1, 92,861 meeting at least one. And \"four final candidates for experimental verification\" from another quote. The item's recorded quote doesn't itself state four; but the claim is supportable from page across sentences. Status: SUPPORTED (or PARTIAL because the note \"most shortlisted molecules never get a physics-based check\" — that's an inference but fine). The ref quote given doesn't include the four, but page contains it. I'd say SUPPORTED with quote noting both? The instructions say give the page sentence relied on. We can quote the 92,861 sentence and mention four finalists sentence. I think SUPPORTED.\n\np2:done: LG AI has not run MD; validation is experimental + literature. SUPPORTED.\n\np2:activity: arXiv dates match. SUPPORTED.\n\np2:step: quote present in html: \"Finally, experimental and literature validation confirm the physicochemical properties of selected candidates (Fig. 2 f), ensuring their suitability as single-phase immersion cooling fluids.\" Yes. And the step says five-stage workflow — confirmed. \"after GATE screens billions down to shortlist, chosen few validated\" — yes. SUPPORTED.\n\np1 items: all quotes present. p1:step: quote present. \"screening over 6 million esters and filtering 461 monoesters on predicted dielectric constant, viscosity and thermal conductivity, pick candidates manually\" — supported. Note: paper says \"over 6 million saturated monoesters and dibasic esters\". Fine.\n\np1:activity: \"Published: 11 July 2025\" present. \"no later release from this collaboration was found\" — that's the searcher's claim; not verifiable from page. Hmm. The item includes a negative claim \"no later release was found\" — that's the plan's search claim, arguably not a factual claim needing source (it's a search statement). It's supported by the page for the date. I'd say SUPPORTED. Possibly PARTIAL because \"no later release found\" can't be verified page. But typically these checks treat such as search claim. I'll say SUPPORTED.\n\np1:c1: quote present exactly. Claim \"with no MD verification in between\" — supported by the pipeline description and also the intro about MD unaffordable. SUPPORTED.\n\np1:c2: \"461 monoesters, of which only two are actually synthesized\" — quote says 461 filtered. Two candidates from further manual assessment. Supported. \"rest are never independently verified\" — reasonable. SUPPORTED.\n\np1:c3: quote present: \"It can be seen that predicting these physical properties via MD is time-consuming for a single system and becomes unaffordable in a high-throughput screening scenario with thousands of candidates\". Claim: team's stated reason for not running MD is cost at screening scale. Yes. SUPPORTED.\n\np1:done: supported.\n\np3 items. Need bitstream. Let's fetch."
