    "content": "Title: Refinement of the OPLS Force Field for Thermodynamics and Dynamics of Liquid Alkanes\nURL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9939004/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nTorsion and Lennard-Jones parameters of the OPLS all-atom force field have been refined for describing thermodynamics and dynamics of a wide range of liquid alkanes. Monte Carlo statistical mechanics (MC) and Molecular Dynamics (MD) simulations were carried out. For thermodynamics properties, MC simulations with truncated electrostatic interactions performed very closely to MD simulations with a Verlet neighbor list and the Particle Mesh Ewald algorithm. The average errors in comparison with experimental data for computed properties were improved with the modified force field (OPLS/2020), especially for long-chain alkanes. For liquid densities, heats of vaporization, and free energies of hydration, the average errors are 0.01 g/cm3, 0.2 kcal/mol, and ca. 0.5 kcal/mol, respectively; significant gains were made for relative heats of vaporization of isomeric series. Results for self-diffusion coefficients also reproduce well experimental data for linear alkane liquids up to hexadecane. The new force field is suitable for use in improved modeling of myriad systems of importance in chemistry, biology, and materials science.\n\n...\n\nThe OPLS/2020 densities for liquid alkanes are compared with the corresponding experimental values in Table 4. The average errors are only 0.01 g/cm3 or 2%, and there is essentially perfect accord between the MC and MD-PME results. The MD-Cutoff results tend to be slightly lower than the others but within 0.01 g/cm3. Uncertainties (±1σ) in the computed values are ca. ±0.005 g/cm3. There is also no drift from the experimental densities with increasing chain length; the MC and MD-PME densities for octane through hexadecane differ from the experimental ones by less than 0.005 g/cm3 in all cases. The mean-signed error (MSE) in the computed densities varies from −0.004 to −0.008 g/cm3, indicating that there is no overall bias. However, a compromise was made such that the densities tend to be slightly overestimated for branched alkanes but underestimated for lower linear and cyclic ones.\n\n...\n\nFurther comparison of OPLS-AA and OPLS/2020 is provided in Table 6 for 12 of the smaller alkanes. Giving a higher weight to the description of long-chain hydrocarbons has not worsened the results for shorter linear and branched ones. The mean errors for density and ΔHvap for OPLS/AA of 0.02 g/cm3 and 0.5 kcal/mol have been reduced to 0.01 g/cm3 and 0.2 kcal/mol using OPLS/2020. A goal was to improve the performance for ΔHvap in isomer series. Thus, all the butane, pentane, and hexane isomers were modeled. The problem is compression of the range of computed heats of vaporization compared to experiment. ΔHvap for alkanes decreases with increased branching owing to reduced intermolecular van der Waals attraction with reduced surface area. For example, the experimental ΔHvap for neopentane (2,2-dimethylpentane) is 1.11 kcal/mol lower than for pentane, and the difference is 0.92 kcal/mol for hexane and 2,2-dimethylbutane. However, with OPLS-AA the corresponding differences are 0.16 and 0.51 kcal/mol, respectively. These differences are improved to 0.99 and 0.77 kcal/mol with OPLS/2020. The experimental densities for the isomeric series in Table 6 exhibit comparatively little variation; the range for the hexanes is only 0.01 g/cm3. Both force fields also give a small range, 0.02 g/cm3, for the hexanes, while only OPLS/2020 gives the correct density order for pentane and neopentane, and for butane and isobutane (2-methylpropane). Overall, the results for all alkanes in Table 6 reflect significant improvement with OPLS/2020 over OPLS-AA with decreases in the mean percentage error from 3.1% to 1.8% for liquid densities and from 8.3% to 2.7% for enthalpies of vaporization. For cyclic alkanes versus linear ones with the same number of carbon atoms, the force fields both reproduce the pattern of higher densities and heats of vaporization for the cyclic molecules.\n\n...\n\nThe diffusion coefficient of a liquid simulated under periodic boundary conditions, DPBC, is also system-size dependent.17,31 To adjust for this problem, a simple analytical correction was suggested based upon the replacement of the Oseen tensor in the Kirkwood theory of diffusion constant by the corresponding Ewald summation formula.17,31 The effect of artifacts imposed by periodic boundary conditions often appears stronger when Ewald-based methods are used rather than cutoff-based ones for treating electrostatic interactions.60–62 In order to evaluate this effect on the self-diffusion constant, the correction term in eq 6 is added to the DPBC values calculated from both MD-Cutoff and MD-PME simulations. Table 8 lists the experimental viscosities of the liquid alkanes and the average length of the simulation box used for the correction in eq 6. The values for DPBC and D0 are provided in Table 9. When more than one experimental value for D0 was found, the average is used as the reference value.\n\n#### Table 8.\n\nViscosity η (mPa-s) of Liquid Alkanes at 25 °C and 1 atm and the Average Length (Å) of the Cubic Simulation Box\n\n| η | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Expt. | MD-Cutoff | MD-PME |\n| pentane | 0.245a | 47.7 | 47.6 |\n| hexane | 0.296a | 48.6 | 48.5 |\n| heptane | 0.382b | 50.4 | 50.2 |\n| octane | 0.511a | 52.1 | 51.9 |\n| decane | 0.840c | 55.2 | 55.1 |\n| dodecane | 1.390a | 58.1 | 58.0 |\n| pentadecane | 2.540c | 62.1 | 61.7 |\n| hexadecane | 3.030c | 63.2 | 63.0\n\n---\n\nTitle: Toward realistic computer modeling of paraffin-based composite materials: critical assessment of atomic-scale models of paraffins\nURL: https://doi.org/10.1039/c9ra07325f\nPublished: 2019-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Artyom D. Glova, Igor V. Volgin, Victor M. Nazarychev, Sergey V. Larin, Sergey V. Lyulin, Andrey A. Gurtovenko\nHighlights:\nParaffin-based composites represent a promising class of materials with numerous practical applications such as e.g. heat storage. Computer modeling of these complex multicomponent systems requires a proper theoretical description of both the n-alkane matrix and the non-alkane filler molecules. The latter can be modeled with the use of a state-of-the-art general-purpose force field such as GAFF, CHARMM, OPLS-AA and GROMOS, while the paraffin matrix is traditionally described in the frame of relatively old, alkane-specific force fields (TraPPE, NERD, and PYS). In this paper we link these two types of models and evaluate the performance of several general-purpose force fields in computer modeling of paraffin by their systematic comparison with earlier alkane-specific models as well as with experimental data. To this end, we have performed molecular dynamics simulations of n-eicosane bulk samples with the use of 10 different force fields: TraPPE, NERD, PYS, OPLS-UA, GROMOS, GAFF, GAFF2, OPLS-AA, L-OPLS-AA, and CHARMM36. For each force field we calculated several thermal, structural and dynamic characteristics of n-eicosane over a wide temperature range. Overall, our findings show that the general-purpose force fields such as CHARMM36, L-OPLS-AA and GAFF/GAFF2 are able to provide a realistic description of n-eicosane samples. While alkane-specific models outperform most general-purpose force fields as far as the temperature dependence of mass density, the coefficient of volumetric thermal expansion in the liquid state, and the crystallization temperature are concerned, L-OPLS-AA, CHARMM36 and GAFF2 force fields provide a better match with experiment for the shear viscosity and the diffusion coefficient in melt. Furthermore, we show that most general-purpose force fields are able to reproduce qualitatively the experimental triclinic crystal structure of n-eicosane at low temperatures.\n\n...\n\nThe OPLS (Optimized\n\n...\n\nThe shear viscosity h is another important characteristic of an eicosane sample in the liquid state, which can also be linked to experimental data. In equilibrium MD simulations the shear viscosity can be calculated according to the Green-Kubo approach by integrating the autocorrelation function S ACF (t) of the non-diagonal components of the pressure tensor: 82, 83 h ¼ lim\n\nt/N V k B T ð t 0 S ACF ðtÞdt (4)\n\nTo calculate the shear viscosity from eqn ( 4 ), here we used a time decomposition method proposed recently by Zhang et al. 69 This method implies a large number of short independent simulation runs to improve the reliability of the viscosity calculation. Correspondingly, for each force eld we prepared 99 initial congurations of an n-eicosane sample at T ¼ 450 K on the basis of three 100 ns trajectories of cooling-rate simulations (33 congurations were extracted from each trajectory). For each initial conguration we performed a 5 ns-long MD simulation that was used then for calculating the shear viscosity. A special care has been taken to ensure that 99 simulation runs provided an accurate measurement of the viscosity (data not shown).\n\nIn Table 2 we list the calculated values of the shear viscosity for each force eld along with the experimental value 84 measured at T ¼ 453 K. It is seen that most united-atom models (except OPLS-UA) underestimate the shear viscosity. The closest match to the experimental data is observed for PYS and GRO-MOS force elds, whose viscosities are 10% and 14% smaller than what was measured in experiment. 84 In turn, the largest deviation from the experimental value of viscosity is seen for OPLS-UA (33%) and NERD (46%) force elds, see Table 2 .\n\nIn turn, all-atom force elds are found to suit much better for describing the shear viscosity of eicosane in the liquid state as compared to the united-atom counter-parts: GAFF2, CHARMM36 and L-OPLS-AA force elds demonstrate an excellent agreement with experimental data, see Table 2 . Two other all-atom force elds (GAFF and OPLS-AA) show considerable deviations (around 30%) from the experimental value for the shear viscosity of eicosane melt (Table 2 ).\n\n...\n\nIn Table 2 we present the diffusion coefficients calculated for all considered force elds. We also included the experimental value of D, which was measured in n-eicosane melt at T ¼ 443 K by the pulsed-gradient spin-echo NMR method. 85 Note that different experimental techniques can give the diffusion coef-cients that scatter considerably. For instance, the diffusion coefficient of n-eicosane chains at T ¼ 423 K was found to be 1.1 Â 10 À5 in NMR experiments and 2.0 Â 10 À5 cm 2 s À1 when the time-of-ight quasielastic neutron scattering was used. 86 evertheless, as one can see from Table 1 , the diffusion coef-cients computed for all force elds are of the same order as the experimental value. 85 In general, all-atom models provide a closer match to the experimental data as compared to their united-atom counter-parts. The best agreement with experiment is achieved when L-OPLS-AA, CHARMM36 and GAFF2 force elds are used. In turn, the GAFF (OPLS-AA) force eld overestimates (underestimates) the experimental value of the diffusion coefficient. Interestingly, most united-atom models (except OPLS-UA) overestimate the diffusion coefficient, see Table 2 . This can be due to the implicit treatment of hydrogen atoms in these force elds, which weakens inter-atomic cohesive interactions and speeds up the dynamics. 87 Among the united-atom models, the best agreement with experiment is seen for PYS and GROMOS force elds. As a concluding remark, it has to be emphasized that the results for the shear viscosity and diffusion are closely related to each other: the larger the viscosity, the slower the diffusion.\n\n...\n\nFor the shear viscosity the best performance was shown by all-atom models (GAFF2, CHARMM36, and L-OPLS-AA). In turn, almost all united-atom models underestimate the shear viscosity of n-eicosane in the liquid state. The results for the viscosity are closely related to the diffusion coefficients of neicosane chains in the liquid state: the larger the viscosity, the slower the diffusion. Therefore, it is not surprising that most united-atom force elds give the diffusion coefficients that exceed the experimental value. Among all-atom models, the best agreement with experiment is observed for GAFF2, CHARMM36, and L-OPLS-AA force elds. Finally, we showed that most computational models considered are able to reproduce qualitatively the experimentally observed triclinic crystal structure of n-eicosane at low temperatures. The only exception is the GROMOS force eld that was not able to describe both the crystallization process and the crystal structure of eicosane samples within the considered temperature range (200-450 K) at cooling rates currently accessible in atomic-scale simulations.\n\n...\n\nThe shear viscosity, the diffusion coefficient and the mass density for n-eicosane samples in the liquid state simulated with different force fields at T ¼ 450 K. The errors are calculated as standard errors of the mean over three independent MD trajectories Force eld | h, mPa s | D, 10 À5 cm 2 s À1 | r, kg m À3 GAFF | 0.42 AE 0.04 | 2.9 AE 0.2 | 592.3 AE 0.1 GAFF2 | 0.59 AE 0.01 | 2.1 AE 0.1 | 633.5 AE 0.1 OPLS-AA | 0.83 AE 0.07 | 1.6 AE 0.1 | 668.2 AE 0.1 L-OPLS-AA | 0.64 AE 0.08 | 2.2 AE 0.2 | 656.4 AE 0.1 CHARMM36 | 0.60 AE 0.04 | 2.1 AE 0.2 | 658.1 AE 0.1 GROMOS | 0.51 AE 0.01 | 2.5 AE 0.1 | 700.9 AE 0.1 NERD | 0.32 AE 0.01 | 3.6 AE 0.1 | 661.0 AE 0.1 OPLS-UA | 0.79 AE 0.02 | 1.8 AE 0.1 | 753.1 AE 0.1 PYS | 0.53 AE 0.01 | 2.6 AE 0.1 | 698.8 AE 0.1 TraPPE | 0.45 AE 0.04 | 2.9 AE 0.1 | 693.7 AE 0.1 Experiment | 0.594 (T ¼ 453 K)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Optimization of the OPLS-AA\nForce Field for Long Hydrocarbons | Journal of Chemical Theory and Computation | ACS Publications\nURL: https://pubs.acs.org/jctcce/article/8/4/1459/1658306/Optimization-of-the-OPLS-AA-Force-Field-for-Long\nPublished: 2012-03-10T01:06:11.000Z\nAuthor: Siu, Shirley W. I., Pluhackova, Kristyna, Böckmann, Rainer A.\nHighlights:\nThe all-atom optimized potentials for liquid simulations (OPLS-AA) force field is a popular force field for simulating biomolecules. However, the current OPLS parameters for hydrocarbons developed using short alkanes cannot reproduce the liquid properties of long alkanes in molecular dynamics simulations. Therefore, the extension of OPLS-AA to (phospho)lipid molecules required for the study of biological membranes was hampered in the past. Here, we optimized the OPLS-AA force field for both short and long hydrocarbons. Following the framework of the OPLS-AA parametrization, we refined the torsional parameters for hydrocarbons by fitting to the gas-phase ab initio energy profiles calculated at the accurate MP2/aug-cc-pVTZ theory level. Additionally, the depth of the Lennard-Jones potential for methylene hydrogen atoms was adjusted to reproduce the densities and the heats of vaporization of alkanes and alkenes of different lengths. Optimization of partial charges finally allowed to reproduce the gel-to-liquid-phase transition temperature for pentadecane and solvation free energies. It is shown that the optimized parameter set (L-OPLS) yields improved hydrocarbon diffusion coefficients, viscosities, and gauche–trans ratios. Moreover, its applicability for lipid bilayer simulations is shown for a GMO bilayer in its liquid-crystalline phase.\n\n---\n\nTitle: OPLS/2020 Force Field for Unsaturated Hydrocarbons,\nAlcohols, and Ethers | The Journal of Physical Chemistry B | ACS Publications\nURL: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/abs/10.1021/acs.jpcb.3c06602\nPublished: 2023-12-21T05:03:34.000Z\nAuthor: Jorgensen, William L., Ghahremanpour, Mohammad M., Saar, Anastasia, Tirado-Rives, Julian\nHighlights:\nThe OPLS all-atom force field was updated and applied to modeling unsaturated hydrocarbons, alcohols, and ethers. Testing has included gas-phase conformational energetics, properties of pure liquids, and free energies of hydration. Monte Carlo statistical mechanics (MC) calculations were used to model 60 liquids. In addition, a robust, automated procedure was devised to compute the free energies of hydration with high precision via free-energy perturbation (FEP) calculations using double annihilation. Testing has included larger molecules than in the past, and parameters are reported for the first time for some less common groups including alkynes, allenes, dienes, and acetals. The average errors in comparison with experimental data for the computed properties of the pure liquids were improved with the modified force field (OPLS/2020). For liquid densities and heats of vaporization, the average unsigned errors are 0.01 g/cm 3 and 0.2 kcal/mol. The average error and signed error for free energies of hydration are both 1.2 kcal/mol. As noted before, this reflects a systematic overestimate of the hydrophobicity of organic molecules when the parametrization is done to minimize the errors for properties of pure liquids. Implications for the modeling of biomolecular systems with standard force fields are considered.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Molecular Dynamics Simulation of Pure n -Alkanes and Their Mixtures at Elevated Temperatures Using Atomistic and Coarse-Grained Force Fields - SCIENOMICS\nURL: https://www.scienomics.com/molecular-dynamics-simulation-of-pure-n-alkanes-and-their-mixtures-at-elevated-temperatures-using-atomistic-and-coarse-grained-force-fields/\nPublished: 2022-02-01T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: admin@scienomics.com\nHighlights:\nThe properties of higher n-alkanes and their mixtures is a topic of significant interest for the oil and chemical industry. However, the experimental data at high temperatures are scarce. The present study focuses on simulating n-dodecane, n-octacosane, their binary mixture at a n-dodecane mole fraction of 0.3, and a model mixture of the commercially available hydrocarbon wax SX-70 to evaluate the performance of several force fields on the reproduction of properties such as liquid densities, surface tension, and viscosities. Molecular dynamics simulations over a broad temperature range from 323.15 to 573.15 K were employed in examining a broad set of atomistic molecular models assessed for the reproduction of experimental data. The well-established united atom TraPPE (TraPPE-UA) was compared against the all atom optimized potentials for liquid simulations (OPLS) reparametrization for long n-alkanes, L-OPLS, as well as Lipid14 and MARTINI force fields. All models qualitatively reproduce the temperature dependence of the aforementioned properties, but TraPPE-UA was found to reproduce liquid densities most accurately and consistently over the entire temperature range. TraPPE-UA and MARTINI were very successful in reproducing surface tensions, and L-OPLS was found to be the most accurate in reproducing the measured viscosities as compared to the other models. Our simulations show that these widely used force fields originating from the world of biomolecular simulations are suitable candidates in the study of n-alkane properties, both in the pure and mixture states.\n\n---\n\nTitle: A tiny charge‐scaling in the OPLS‐AA + L‐OPLS force field delivers the realistic dynamics and structure of liquid primary alcohols\nURL: https://doi.org/10.1002/jcc.26802\nPublished: 2021-12-28T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Xuan Zhang, Yongtao Wang, Jia Yao, Haoran Li, Kenji Mochizuki\nHighlights:\n## Abstract\n\nWe carry out molecular dynamics simulations for pure liquid primary alcohols ranging from methanol to 1-decanol under ambient conditions. Based on the OPLS-AA force field with the L-OPLS correction, we demonstrate that a few % increases in the partial charges deliver the realistic dynamics (self-diffusion coefficient and shear viscosity) and structure (density and X-ray scattering intensity) as well as enthalpy of vaporization and isothermal compressibility. The validity against thermal expansion coefficient, isobaric heat capacity, and static dielectric constant are also discussed."
   },
   {
    "role": "tool",
    "tool_call_id": "call_01_c2tqabWTb1lVrsa2ND3f1653",
    "content": "Title: Discovery\nURL: https://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/fa07c113-8958-4f1e-a010-0ef2bfc39e17/content\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nBenchmarking Machine Learning and Molecular\n\nDynamics Methods for the Prediction of the\n\nThermophysical Properties of Organic Liquids\n\nEgheosa Ogbomo,† ,‡ Fionn Carman,∗ ,† ,‡ Daniele Dini,† and James P. Ewen∗ ,† ,¶\n\n† Department of Mechanical Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London SW7 2AZ, U.K. ‡ These authors contributed equally ¶ Department of Mechanical Engineering, University of Bath, Claverton Down, Bath BA2 7AY, U.K.\n\nE-mail: fionn.carman18@imperial.ac.uk; j.ewen@imperial.ac.uk\n\nAbstract\n\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4,700 critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10–C50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representa tions. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range identify higher-risk regions at the population level, rather than ordering the errors of individual molecules. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD\n\n...\n\nis most valuable beyond the chemistries that have already been measured, we then examine how far that accuracy extends to molecules unlike those seen in training, and which signals available at prediction time indicate where it does not. Finally, we apply Green–Kubo MD simulations to eight molecules, two from each of the four principal chemical families of the dataset, at 40 °C and 100 °C. This extends force field benchmarks that have concentrated on linear and branched alkanes 26,28,29 to alkyl cycloalkanes, alkyl aromatics, esters, and ethers, for which experimental viscosity data remain sparse 10 despite their importance in automotive fuels, 70 aviation fuels, 71 and lubricants. 9,72\n\n...\n\nFigure 2: The eight MD target molecules, two from each principal chemical family of lubricant base oils in the dataset: alkanes (squalane, nonylcyclohexane), alkyl aromatics (4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, 2-(1-propylheptyl)naphthalene), esters (decyl stearate, bis(2ethylhexyl) sebacate), and polyethers (7,10,13-trioxanonadecane, tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether).\n\nThe molecular structures of the eight molecules selected for this benchmarking study are shown in Figure 2. These molecules were chosen to span the principal chemical fami lies encountered in the reference dataset used in this work. All the MD simulations were performed using the LAMMPS software. 102 EMD simulations were employed to calculate the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of the eight target molecules at 40 ◦C and 100 ◦C, consistent with conditions used to determine lubricant viscosity index values. 8 All\n\nsimulations were performed using the LOPLS-AA force field. 103 Parameters for the LOPLS simulations were taken from Jorgensen et al. 104 and Siu et al. 103 Where required, the ester extension to LOPLS-AA was employed, 105 while aromatic and ether parameters were taken from the corresponding OPLS-AA parameterizations. LOPLS-AA was selected because pre vious benchmarks have demonstrated it to be among the most accurate all-atom force fields for reproducing the experimental density and viscosity of linear hydrocarbons, including n-hexadecane, 28 n-dodecane, and n-octacosane. 29\n\n...\n\nWe complement the ML models with EMD simulations of the eight target molecules shown \nin Figure 2, spanning the four principal chemical families in the reference dataset with two \nmolecules from each. Density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity were computed at 40 and \n100 ◦C with the LOPLS-AA force field, 103 assigned directly from each molecule’s SMILES \nstring using our open-source builder tool. The same properties were predicted with the \nbest-performing XGBoost descriptor models identified in the ML benchmarking above. The \ncomparison is not intended to adjudicate one method against the other, but to characterize \nMD as an independent, physics-based route to these properties and to weigh the strengths \nand limitations of each. Per-molecule MD, ML, and reference values for all three properties \nat both temperatures are tabulated in Table S14, Table S15, and Table S16. \nOf the three properties, density is reproduced most accurately. MD matches the reference \nvalues to within 3% for six of the eight molecules at 40 ◦C (within 4% for seven) and within 6% \nfor seven of the eight at 100 ◦C (Figure 7a, Figure S10a), consistent with previous benchmarks \nfor linear and branched alkanes. 26 The instantaneous density reaches a stable plateau within \napproximately 0.2 ns of the 1 ns NPT equilibration (shown for all eight molecules at both \ntemperatures in Figure S11), and no residual equilibration drift is detectable in the reported \nvalues. \nThe exception is tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether, the most oxygen-rich molecule of the \nset, which MD under-predicts by 10% at 40 ◦C and 13% at 100 ◦C. This agreement is ex \npected, since liquid densities are governed largely by the repulsive and dispersion parameters \nand force fields such as OPLS are often directly fitted to reproduce liquid densities. 103,104\n\n...\n\ning force field parameterization because they require longer simulations to calculate. 22 This makes them a more challenging target for transferable MD force fields such as OPLS. Fig ure S12 shows the stress autocorrelation function (Figure S12a) and its running Green–Kubo integral η(t) (Figure S12b) for decyl stearate at 40 ◦C. The slow tail of the autocorrelation means the integral rises over several hundred picoseconds, the double-exponential TDM fit 117 is taken up to the ση/η∞ = 0.4 cutoff, and beyond the cutoff the spread across replicas grows rapidly as statistical noise dominates. To ensure proper convergence, we analyzed η∞ for trajectory lengths between 1 and 8 ns, showing that η∞ plateaus at approximately 6–8 ns for all eight molecules (Figure S13). Accordingly, all production viscosity calculations were performed using 8 ns trajectories to ensure convergence with respect to trajectory length. The accuracy of the force field varies with the chemistry of the molecule (Figure 7b, Fig ure S10b). For the four hydrocarbons, the viscosity deviations from the reference values are generally modest. Squalane deviates by − 4% at 40 ◦C and − 7% at 100 ◦C, nonylcyclohexane by +8% and − 1%, 2-(1-propylheptyl)naphthalene by +12% and +14%, while 4-hexyl-1,7diphenylheptane, under-predicted by 25% at 40 ◦C, recovers to − 5% at 100 ◦C. By contrast, viscosity is under-predicted for all oxygen-containing molecules at both temperatures. The esters by 17–38% (decyl stearate − 38% and − 32%; bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate − 17% and − 25%) and the ethers by 26–57% (7,10,13-trioxanonadecane − 54% and − 57%; tetraethy lene glycol diethyl ether − 26% and − 47%). A similar viscosity under-prediction was also noted for diesters in previous MD simulations 42 with LOPLS. 105 Because the trajectory length sweeps plateau well below the reference values, rather than continuing to rise toward them (Figure S13), these systematic under-predictions are unlikely to arise from incomplete Green–Kubo convergence. Instead, they are consistent with a limitation of the force field for the oxygen-containing molecules in this dataset. With only eight molecules used for the MD benchmarking, these observations can only be used to identify plausible explanations, rather than establish firm conclusions. Despite these limitations, the force field largely pre serves the ordering of the reference viscosity values for the eight fluids at both temperatures,\n\nsuggesting that MD simulations remain useful for relative screening, even where its absolute \nviscosity predictions require caution. \nThe XGBoost viscosity predictions show no comparable chemistry-dependent bias: their \ndeviations scatter in both directions within each family. Their magnitudes are, however, \nlarger than for density or thermal conductivity, and in line with the benchmark’s OOF \nviscosity RMSE of 0.35 in ln(η/mPa s), which corresponds to a one-standard-deviation factor \nof 1.4. Four of the eight molecules lie within 25% of the reference values at 40 ◦C, with \nsqualane under-predicted by 42% and decyl stearate, 4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, and bis(2- \nethylhexyl) sebacate over-predicted by 44, 69, and 138%; at 100 ◦C four lie within 10%, with \ndecyl stearate (+45%), 7,10,13-trioxanonadecane (− 27%), tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether \n(− 37%), and bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate (+144%) the exceptions. \nThe thermal conductivity converges far more quickly in the MD simulations compared to \nthe viscosity, but it carries the opposite bias. As shown in Figure S14a, the autocorrelation of \nthe heat current decays within a few picoseconds, so its running integral κ(t) (Figure S14b) \nreaches an early plateau from which the conductivity is read before noise accumulates at \nlonger times; unlike the viscosity, the estimates are already converged within the first 1–2 ns \nof trajectory (Figure S15). LOPLS over-predicts κ for every molecule at both temperatures, \nwith deviations ranging from +7 to +96% (Figure 7c, Figure S10c). \nThe magnitude of the over-prediction increases with the amount of aliphatic C–H bonds \nacross the eight molecules. The saturated alkanes are worst (squalane +96% at 40 ◦C and \n+87% at 100 ◦C; nonylcyclohexane +70% and +74%), the esters follow (decyl stearate +57% \nand +54%; bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate +40% and +50%), the polyethers are milder (+5 to \n+22%), and the two aromatics, whose hydrogen is carried largely on rigid ring carbons, are \nmildest (+7 to +11%); across the set the deviations correlate with the number of aliphatic \nhydrogens per heavy atom (40 ◦C deviations, n = 8: Pearson r = 0.79, two-sided p = \n0.020; Spearman rs = 0.81, p = 0.015; Figure S16). The two polyethers are the clearest \nexceptions, their thermal conductivity is over-predicted by less than their aliphatic hydrogen \n\ncontent alone would imply. This ordering is consistent with a vibrational contribution. Hydrocarbons contain C–H bonds that produce very fast, high-frequency intramolecular stretching and bending vibrations. Standard classical force fields often over-exaggerate the coupling and propagation of energy through these stiff covalent modes without adequate anharmonic damping, leading to a significant overestimation of heat flux. 111 While this can be improved by constraining the C–H bonds with SHAKE, 110 (as done in our MD simulations) this removes only the stretching contribution, leaving the bending and torsional modes of the aliphatic hydrogen atoms classical and over-populated. 111 The introduction of oxygen atoms introduces lone pairs and heavier local masses, shifting the vibrational spectrum. Dipolar oxygen centers increase local vibrational scattering and disrupt long-range phonon like coherent transfer along the backbone, lowering the simulated thermal conductivity and leaving it closer to the reference values. 140 In practice, at 40 ◦C the simulations compress the reference range of 0.12–0.21 W m− 1K− 1\n\ninto a band of 0.19–0.25 W m− 1K− 1 and misorder the molecules, placing squalane, which the \nreference values place as the least conductive of the eight, among the most conductive. \nUnlike the viscosity, the LOPLS force field does not reproduce even the relative ordering of \nthe thermal conductivities of the eight fluids. The OOF XGBoost values for this set stay \nwithin 8% of the reference values at both temperatures and preserve the ordering. \nFigure 8 shows the mean absolute deviation from the reference values for the eight \nmolecules. The two approaches separate cleanly only for thermal conductivity, where the \nsimulations deviate by 39% and every ML model stays below 3%. For viscosity at 40 °C the \nforce field and the XGBoost descriptor model are each closer for four of the eight molecules, \nand density is predicted to within a few percent by both. \nThe two approaches also differ greatly in cost. Converging the transport properties of one \nmolecule at one state point required ten replicas of up to 8 ns each; at the 2.4–3.6 ns day− 1 \nour systems of ≈ 15,000 atoms achieve on 24 cores, this corresponds to roughly 1.5 × 10 4\n\n...\n\nIn this study, we built and benchmarked ML models for the prediction of the density, viscos \nity, and thermal conductivity of fuel- and lubricant-sized organic molecules, and used MD \nsimulations as an independent, physics-based assessment on a representative subset. We \nevaluated 26 ML model–representation combinations under a shared nested cross-validation \nprotocol on a new critically evaluated reference dataset of 835 molecules, and applied Green– \nKubo MD simulations to eight target molecules, two from each of the principal chemical \nfamilies in the dataset, at 40 and 100 °C. \nOur evaluation of molecular representations highlights the importance of representation– \nmodel compatibility. PCA-based embeddings outperformed UMAP across most properties \nand model types, and unhashed Morgan fingerprints with large radii eliminated collision \ninduced ambiguity and gave the best fingerprint performance; nevertheless, full molecular \ndescriptors remained the strongest representation overall. These two representation compar \nisons were made on a single split rather than under the nested protocol, and are conditional \non it. XGBoost with descriptors gave the lowest viscosity error of any combination and was \nalso among the best models for density and thermal conductivity, with random forests on \ndescriptors best for density and frozen ChemBERTa embeddings with an MLP head best \nfor thermal conductivity at 40 °C. Distance-based models with descriptors followed closely, \nand a chemistry-pretrained transformer was among the strongest models when its frozen \nembeddings were used directly. \nThe MD benchmark, using a Green–Kubo protocol with quantified trajectory-length con \nvergence, shows that the accuracy of the LOPLS-AA force field is property- and chemistry \n\ndependent. Density is reproduced to within 4% for seven of the eight molecules at 40 °C. \nViscosity errors are small for the pure hydrocarbons with LOPLS, but this property is con \nsistently under-predicted for the oxygen-containing molecules; the reference ranking of the \nfluids is nonetheless largely preserved. Thermal conductivity is over-predicted for every \nmolecule, by amounts that correlate with the density of aliphatic C–H bonds, compressing \nthe reference range and misordering the fluids. \nThe MD case study therefore documents clear differences in force-field accuracy between \nchemical families, although the small number of molecules means the causes of these dif \nferences remain to be firmly established. The ML models, trained directly on the target \nproperties, show no comparable bias across the chemical families of this eight-molecule case \nstudy: their OOF predictions stay close to the reference values for the density and thermal \nconductivity of these molecules, with larger but unsystematic errors for viscosity, at an in \nference cost some seven orders of magnitude below that of an EMD calculation. The two ap \nproaches are therefore suited to different problems: data-driven models for high-throughput \nscreening across the chemistries and conditions where experimental training data exist, and \nphysical simulation for extrapolation beyond them, such as mixtures and elevated pressures, \nwhere MD remains indispensable. \nWe also investigated the OOD prediction capabilities of the ML models, finding that \nextrapolation beyond the target property range seen during training degrades performance \nmore than encountering molecules with novel atom types. This highlights the importance of \ntraining set coverage in both chemical and property space for reliable ML-based screening. \nThe distance and predicted-value signals identify higher-risk regions at the population level \nrather than ordering the errors of individual molecules, and the two-signal rule remains \nexploratory because it was assessed on the same external set used to formulate it. We have \ndemonstrated the ability of ML models to accurately predict the thermophysical properties \nof lubricant base oil-sized molecules, provided that they are trained on high-quality critically \nevaluated reference data for around a thousand unique molecules, which supports their use\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\nURL: https://doi.org/10.1039/d6dd00260a\nPublished: 2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Egheosa Ogbomo, Fionn Carman, Daniele Dini, James Patrick Ewen\nHighlights:\n, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking Machine Learning and Molecular Dynamics Methods for the Prediction of the Thermophysical Properties of Organic Liquids | Digital Discovery | The Royal Society of Chemistry\nURL: https://pubs.rsc.org/dd/article/doi/10.1039/D6DD00260A/1362876/Benchmarking-Machine-Learning-and-Molecular\nPublished: 2026-09-29T03:08:18.000Z\nAuthor: Ogbomo, Egheosa, Carman, Fionn, Dini, Daniele, Ewen, James Patrick\nHighlights:\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4,700 experimental and critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10--C50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representations. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that unreliable predictions can be flagged from descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and Machine Learning Methods for Predicting Hydrocarbon Thermophysical Properties\nURL: https://doi.org/10.26434/chemrxiv.15002312/v1\nPublished: 2026-04-22T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Egheosa Ogbomo, Fionn Carman, Daniele Dini, James Patrick Ewen\nHighlights:\nand machine learning (ML). To train the ML models, we construct a database containing more than 5,500 experimental ... . The XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors consistently achieved the best predictive performance\n\n---\n\nTitle: Benchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and Machine Learning Methods for Predicting Hydrocarbon Thermophysical Properties | OpenAlex\nURL: https://openalex.org/works/w7155155945\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nAbstract: Accurate prediction of the thermophysical properties of hydrocarbons is critical to the rational design of fuels, lubricants, and cooling fluids. We compare the performance of two widely used computat... more\n\n---\n\nTitle: I'm not a regular poster on LinkedIn, but this one is probably worth it. | Egheosa Ogbomo, PhD\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/egheosa-ogbomo-phd-225143ab_benchmarking-molecular-dynamics-simulations-activity-7452702005871112192-nGml\nPublished: 2026-04-22T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Egheosa Ogbomo, PhD\nHighlights:\nOver the course of 18 months during my PhD, we collated 5,500+ experimental data ... ~1,200 hydrocarbons (manually!!), using this data to benchmark various machine learning models against molecular dynamics simulations for predicting density, viscosity,"
